   Below are listed the methods in the OCT system.  There abstract
   functionallity and return types are detailed.  Each class is
   free to interpret them within some leway; after all this is an
   object oriented system with data-hiding! 


        The name in square brackets ([,]) after the method protype
   is the category where the method is first defined.  Generally
   speaking, any method in this category of classes or inheriting
   from a class in this category can respond sucessfully to this
   message.  



   11..11.. FFaaccttoorryy MMeetthhooddss      
   These methods are known by factory objects.  


   iinniittiiaalliizzee [[FFoouunnddaattiioonn]]      Perform any presetting for this
        class.  Returing nil will cause the application to abort.
        Called before user's application begins to run.  Classes
        with complex instance variable declarations that can't be
        encoded during translation may change their text encoding.  

        A handler for cleanup may be installed by calling
        _oc_atExit().  The single parameter is a function that will
        be called in LIFO (stack) order when the application exits.  


   nneeww [[FFoouunnddaattiioonn]]      Allocate and initialize a new instance of
        the receiver class.  In contrast to [Cox86], in a factory
        method, it's illegal to assign the result to the variable
        `self', as that refers to the class object, and not the
        instance object! 


       + new
       {
          id newObj;

          /*  Never assign to "self" in a factory!  */
          newObj = [super new];
          newObj->capacity = 0;
          return( newObj );
       }


   Barney - OCT                  pg. 1                   29-Jul-1993


   


   ((iinntt))nnddxxVVaarrSSiizzee [[FFoouunnddaattiioonn]]      Reply the count of bytes each
        indexed variable occupies. For non-indexing class, the
        default from Object will return 0.  Effectively returns:
        sizeof( [self ndxVarType] ).  


   ((SSTTRR))nnddxxVVaarrTTyyppee [[FFoouunnddaattiioonn]]      Reply a string encoding that
        describes the instance variables that are index-able.  For
        instance, BByytteeAArrrraayy returns "c", char type.  


   ppoosseeAAss::((ccllaassssIIdd)) [[FFoouunnddaattiioonn]]      Perform surgery and change the
        class of the receiver to a new class.  (Still under
        construction.) 


   wwiitthh::((iinntt))nnAArrggss vvaaDDccll::((iidd))aarrggLLiisstt [[FFoouunnddaattiioonn]]     Create a
        "sized" object, capable of holding 'nArgs' things.  Of
        course, the class is free is adjust to account for memory
        management, and additional memory could be used by a class's
        superclass.  The "vaDcl:" selector is inserted automatically
        by the translator when it sees a comma inside a message
        expression.  



   11..22.. IInnssttaannccee MMeetthhooddss      
   These methods are known by instance objects.  


   aadddd::iitteemm [[AArrrraayy aanndd CCoolllleeccttiioonn]]    Store 'item' at the next
        available slot.  If there is a notion of a cursor in self,
        then 'item' will be after the cursored slot.  Array's
        generally store in the first unused (nil) slot.  


   aatt::((iinntt))iinnddeexx ppuutt::((iidd))aannOObbjjeecctt [[FFoouunnddaattiioonn]]     Store an object
        into the indexing storage receiver object.  


   ((iinntt)) ccoouunntt [[AArrrraayy aanndd CCoolllleeccttiioonn]]   Return the count of usable
        objects contained within the object.  For Array types, 'nil'
        pointers are not counted.  (Was named 'size'.) 


   eerrrroorr::((SSTTRR))ffmmttSSTTRR vvaaDDccll::ssttuuffff [[FFoouunnddaattiioonn]]     Display an error
        message about self.  Feeds 'stuff' to an sprintf() call.
        Aborts the program and never returns.  






   Barney - OCT                  pg. 2                   29-Jul-1993


   


   ffiinndd::ootthheerrOObbjj [[AArrrraayy aanndd CCoolllleeccttiioonn]]    Returns a member that is
        "==" to 'otherObj'.  Returns nil if there is no match.  


   ffiinnddMMaattcchhiinngg::ootthheerrOObbjj [[AArrrraayy aanndd CCoolllleeccttiioonn]]    Returns a member
        that is isEqual: to 'otherObj'.  Returns nil if there is no
        match.  


   ffiinnddSSTTRR::((SSTTRR))aaSSttrriinngg [[AArrrraayy aanndd CCoolllleeccttiioonn]]    Returns a member
        that is isEqual: to 'aString'.  Returns nil if there is no
        match.  


   ffiirrsstt [[SSeeqquueennccee]]      Sets the objects cursor to be the first
        element.  Returns the first element within the list.  This
        is how sequencing starts.  Performing [list next] returns
        the second element.  


   ffrreeee [[FFoouunnddaattiioonn]]      Releases memory used by object and returns
        nil.  The object is no longer usable, and any references the
        appliation holds must be forgotten.  Because there is no
        reference counting, free'ing an object that has an
        infrastructure (like SSeett)) will create unreachable objects.  


   ffrreeeeCCoonntteennttss [[FFoouunnddaattiioonn]]      Walks through internal contents
        and frees any major sub-objects.  The internal
        infrastructure is invisible to the application.  Added an
        object after freeContents will cause the object to rebuild
        its infrastructure.  


   hhaasshh [[FFoouunnddaattiioonn]]      Return an integer representing a the
        receiver in hashed form.  The same hash value is returned
        for all objects that are YES for "isEqual:".  Ie, [obj1
        hash] == [obj2 hash], iff [obj1 isSame:obj2] == YES.
        Implemented in OObbjjeecctt so everything hash a hash value.
        Re-implemented in classes like BByytteeAArrrraayy..  


   llaasstt [[CCoolllleeccttiioonn]]      Moves a cursor to the last element stored
        and returns that element.  


   ttooFFiirrsstt [[CCoolllleeccttiioonn]]      Set a cursor inside the object to point
        at the first element.  Returns 'self', not the first object
        in the list.  Performing [list next] returns the first
        element.  




   Barney - OCT                  pg. 3                   29-Jul-1993


   


   ttooLLaasstt [[CCoolllleeccttiioonn]]      Set a cursor inside the object to point
        just past the last object.  Returns 'self', not the last
        object in the list.  Performing [list prev] returns the last
        element.  


















































